WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.656
AI-assisted review works best when the
reviewer brings a pattern library.

2
00:00:07.736 --> 00:00:15.006
For input validation, look for untrusted
data entering from HTTP requests,

3
00:00:15.086 --> 00:00:23.378
message queues, files, command-line
arguments, environment variables, callbacks,

4
00:00:23.458 --> 00:00:25.730
and external services.

5
00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:31.603
Ask whether the code validates type,
length, format, range,

6
00:00:31.683 --> 00:00:34.637
and allowed values before use.

7
00:00:34.717 --> 00:00:42.441
For output encoding, check whether data is
encoded for the correct destination,

8
00:00:42.521 --> 00:00:48.996
such as HTML, JavaScript, SQL, shell
commands, logs, or file paths.

9
00:00:49.076 --> 00:00:55.778
Access control and authentication deserve
careful human attention.

10
00:00:55.858 --> 00:01:01.423
AI can point out missing checks or
inconsistent patterns,

11
00:01:01.503 --> 00:01:07.410
but the reviewer must understand roles,
ownership, tenancy,

12
00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:14.192
object-level access, session behavior, and
framework conventions.

13
00:01:14.272 --> 00:01:20.292
A function may look protected because it
is behind middleware,

14
00:01:20.372 --> 00:01:26.393
or it may look exposed because the
protection lives elsewhere.

15
00:01:26.473 --> 00:01:32.948
Review session management, password reset
flows, token handling,

16
00:01:33.028 --> 00:01:41.206
authorization helpers, and admin-only
actions with the actual application routing

17
00:01:41.286 --> 00:01:43.899
and configuration in mind.

18
00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:51.589
Other recurring patterns include
cryptography misuse, unsafe file handling,

19
00:01:51.669 --> 00:01:59.053
command execution, deserialization,
dependency risk, and error handling.

20
00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:06.062
AI may help identify suspicious APIs, weak
defaults, or missing checks,

21
00:02:06.142 --> 00:02:10.686
but it can also overstate risk without
context.

22
00:02:10.766 --> 00:02:16.104
For each pattern, ask what input controls
the behavior,

23
00:02:16.184 --> 00:02:21.978
what security property is at stake, what
guard should exist,

24
00:02:22.058 --> 00:02:25.238
and where the evidence is in code.

25
00:02:25.318 --> 00:02:32.020
A pattern is a starting point for
analysis, not a conclusion by itself.
